{
  "questions": [
    {
      "area": "Core product",
      "question": "What is 'Thin API' — what does it actually do?",
      "options": [
        {"label": "A", "description": "A minimal HTTP/REST passthrough layer that fronts an internal service or LLM, exposing only a small, opinionated surface area to external/internal consumers.", "tradeoff": "Fast to ship, easy to reason about, but requires upstream service to do heavy lifting."},
        {"label": "B", "description": "A BFF (backend-for-frontend) that aggregates multiple internal services into a single API tailored to one client.", "tradeoff": "Better DX for the client, but couples the API to client needs and adds aggregation complexity."},
        {"label": "C", "description": "An API gateway / adapter that normalizes vendor APIs (e.g., model providers, payment providers) behind a common interface.", "tradeoff": "Insulates callers from vendor churn, but adds a translation layer to maintain."}
      ],
      "recommendation": "Option A — 'Thin API' most naturally reads as a minimal passthrough; pick this unless the team has signaled otherwise."
    },
    {
      "area": "Phase scope",
      "question": "What does Phase 3 add on top of Phases 1 and 2?",
      "options": [
        {"label": "A", "description": "Hardening: auth, rate limiting, observability, error envelopes — moving from prototype to production-ready.", "tradeoff": "Highest value if Phases 1–2 were happy-path only; less exciting than new features."},
        {"label": "B", "description": "New endpoints / capabilities expanding the API surface (e.g., streaming, batch, new resources).", "tradeoff": "Visible feature progress, but risky if foundation is shaky."},
        {"label": "C", "description": "Multi-tenant / customer-facing release: SDKs, API keys, billing hooks, public docs.", "tradeoff": "Unlocks external usage, but a large lift across many surfaces."}
      ],
      "recommendation": "Option A — Phase 3 typically follows a build-then-harden cadence; default to hardening unless told otherwise."
    },
    {
      "area": "Users",
      "question": "Who calls this API?",
      "options": [
        {"label": "A", "description": "Internal services and one first-party frontend only.", "tradeoff": "Simpler auth (service tokens, session cookies); no public docs needed."},
        {"label": "B", "description": "External developers / partners with API keys.", "tradeoff": "Requires key management, quotas, public reference docs, versioning discipline."}
      ],
      "recommendation": "Option A — start internal-only; promote to external in a later phase if needed."
    },
    {
      "area": "Auth",
      "question": "How are callers authenticated and authorized?",
      "options": [
        {"label": "A", "description": "Bearer tokens (JWT) issued by an existing identity provider; per-route scope checks.", "tradeoff": "Standard, integrates with existing auth; requires a working IdP."},
        {"label": "B", "description": "Static API keys per service, rotated manually.", "tradeoff": "Simplest possible; weak rotation story and limited per-user context."}
      ],
      "recommendation": "Option A — JWT bearer tokens with route-level scopes is the right default for an internal thin API."
    },
    {
      "area": "Tech stack",
      "question": "What runtime and framework?",
      "options": [
        {"label": "A", "description": "TypeScript + Hono (or Fastify) on Node, deployed as a container.", "tradeoff": "Strong typing, fast cold starts, ecosystem-rich."},
        {"label": "B", "description": "Python + FastAPI.", "tradeoff": "Excellent if downstream is Python/ML; slower cold starts."},
        {"label": "C", "description": "Go + chi/echo.", "tradeoff": "Best performance, smallest footprint; smaller team familiarity."}
      ],
      "recommendation": "Option A — TypeScript + Hono is a strong default for a thin passthrough; revise if downstream is Python-heavy."
    },
    {
      "area": "Observability",
      "question": "What instrumentation ships in Phase 3?",
      "options": [
        {"label": "A", "description": "Structured JSON logs + OpenTelemetry traces + RED metrics (rate/error/duration) per route, exported to existing collector.", "tradeoff": "Full picture; some setup cost."},
        {"label": "B", "description": "Logs only; defer tracing.", "tradeoff": "Faster to ship; harder to debug latency issues later."}
      ],
      "recommendation": "Option A — observability is the whole point of a hardening phase."
    },
    {
      "area": "Rate limiting & quotas",
      "question": "How are abusive or runaway callers contained?",
      "options": [
        {"label": "A", "description": "Per-token sliding-window rate limit in Redis, with 429 + Retry-After.", "tradeoff": "Standard, predictable; needs Redis."},
        {"label": "B", "description": "No limits in Phase 3 — rely on upstream quotas.", "tradeoff": "Less infra; one bad caller can take the API down."}
      ],
      "recommendation": "Option A — sliding-window per-token in Redis."
    },
    {
      "area": "Error contract",
      "question": "What does an error response look like?",
      "options": [
        {"label": "A", "description": "RFC 7807 problem+json with stable error codes, request IDs, and machine-readable details.", "tradeoff": "Self-documenting, debuggable; small migration cost from ad-hoc shapes."},
        {"label": "B", "description": "Free-form `{error: string}` shape.", "tradeoff": "Easy now; painful to evolve clients later."}
      ],
      "recommendation": "Option A — RFC 7807 with stable codes."
    },
    {
      "area": "Versioning",
      "question": "How are breaking changes managed?",
      "options": [
        {"label": "A", "description": "URI versioning (`/v1/...`) with one supported version at a time during Phase 3.", "tradeoff": "Simple; defers multi-version pain."},
        {"label": "B", "description": "Header-based content negotiation.", "tradeoff": "More flexible; more client-side complexity."}
      ],
      "recommendation": "Option A — `/v1/` URI prefix, one version supported."
    },
    {
      "area": "Out of scope",
      "question": "What is explicitly NOT in Phase 3?",
      "options": [
        {"label": "A", "description": "Public/external developer access, SDKs, billing, multi-region deployment, GraphQL.", "tradeoff": "Keeps Phase 3 focused on hardening; defers external-facing work."}
      ],
      "recommendation": "Option A — explicitly defer external developer surface and SDKs."
    },
    {
      "area": "Success criteria",
      "question": "How do we know Phase 3 shipped?",
      "options": [
        {"label": "A", "description": "All routes covered by JWT auth + rate limits + structured logs + traces; p95 latency under target; error rate under target; on-call runbook exists; first-party frontend migrated to /v1.", "tradeoff": "Concrete, measurable; demands instrumentation be wired before claiming done."}
      ],
      "recommendation": "Option A — measurable hardening checklist plus first-party frontend cutover."
    }
  ],
  "recommendations": [
    {"area": "Core product", "recommendation": "Thin API is a minimal HTTP passthrough fronting internal services with an opinionated, narrow surface area.", "rationale": "Matches the name; aligns with how 'thin API' is conventionally understood."},
    {"area": "Phase scope", "recommendation": "Phase 3 = production hardening (auth, rate limits, observability, error contract, versioning) plus first-party frontend migration to /v1.", "rationale": "Phase numbering after a working prototype almost always means hardening."},
    {"area": "Users", "recommendation": "Internal services and the first-party frontend only; no external developers.", "rationale": "Avoids scope creep into key management and public docs."},
    {"area": "Auth", "recommendation": "JWT bearer tokens from the existing IdP, validated at the edge, with per-route scope checks.", "rationale": "Standard, secure, integrates with existing identity."},
    {"area": "Tech stack", "recommendation": "TypeScript + Hono on Node, container-deployed.", "rationale": "Right balance of types, perf, and ecosystem for a thin passthrough."},
    {"area": "Observability", "recommendation": "Structured JSON logs, OpenTelemetry traces, and RED metrics per route.", "rationale": "Hardening without observability is not hardening."},
    {"area": "Rate limiting", "recommendation": "Per-token sliding-window in Redis; 429 with Retry-After.", "rationale": "Prevents single-caller incidents; predictable client behavior."},
    {"area": "Error contract", "recommendation": "RFC 7807 problem+json with stable error codes and request IDs.", "rationale": "Stable, debuggable, evolvable."},
    {"area": "Versioning", "recommendation": "URI versioning under /v1; one supported version during Phase 3.", "rationale": "Lowest-friction strategy at this stage."},
    {"area": "Out of scope", "recommendation": "External developer access, public SDKs, billing, multi-region, GraphQL, new business endpoints.", "rationale": "Keeps the phase tightly scoped to hardening."},
    {"area": "Success", "recommendation": "All routes hardened (auth + limits + observability + 7807 errors), p95/error SLOs met, runbook written, first-party frontend cut over to /v1.", "rationale": "Measurable, all-or-nothing exit criteria."}
  ],
  "rewritten_braindump": "# Thin API — Phase 3 (Production Hardening)\n\n## Core product\n\nThe Thin API is a minimal HTTP service that exposes a narrow, opinionated surface area in front of our internal services. It does not own business logic — it authenticates callers, validates inputs, forwards to upstream services, and shapes responses into a stable contract. Phases 1 and 2 delivered the happy-path passthrough and the initial route set. Phase 3 takes that prototype and makes it production-ready, then migrates the first-party frontend onto the hardened `/v1` surface.\n\n## Users\n\n- Internal backend services calling the API service-to-service.\n- The first-party web frontend, via session-derived JWTs.\n\nNo external developers, no partner integrations, no public SDKs in this phase.\n\n## Scope of Phase 3\n\n1. **Auth**: JWT bearer tokens issued by the existing identity provider, validated at the edge of every route. Per-route scope checks. Service-to-service callers use service-account tokens from the same IdP.\n2. **Rate limiting**: Per-token sliding-window limiter backed by Redis. Exceeded requests return `429` with a `Retry-After` header. Defaults are conservative; per-route overrides supported via config.\n3. **Error contract**: All non-2xx responses follow RFC 7807 (`application/problem+json`) with a stable `code`, human `title`, `detail`, `request_id`, and machine-readable `errors[]` for validation failures.\n4. **Observability**: Structured JSON logs (one line per request, with `request_id`, `route`, `status`, `duration_ms`, `caller`), OpenTelemetry traces propagated to upstream services, and RED metrics (rate, errors, duration) per route exported to the existing collector.\n5. **Versioning**: All routes live under `/v1/`. Only one version is supported during Phase 3. Breaking changes require `/v2/`, which is explicitly a future phase.\n6. **Input validation**: All request bodies and query parameters validated against typed schemas at the boundary; validation failures return 7807 errors.\n7. **Frontend cutover**: The first-party web frontend migrates from the legacy/Phase-2 endpoints to `/v1` and adopts the new error contract.\n8. **On-call readiness**: Runbook covering top alerts (5xx rate, p95 latency, dependency outage, rate-limit storms), dashboards linked, paging configured.\n\n## Tech stack\n\n- TypeScript on Node, framework: Hono.\n- Redis for rate limiting and short-lived caches.\n- OpenTelemetry SDK + existing collector.\n- Container-deployed via the existing platform; single region.\n\n## Out of scope (explicitly deferred)\n\n- External developer access, public API keys, partner onboarding.\n- Public SDKs and reference docs site.\n- Billing, metering, plan tiers.\n- Multi-region deployment and active-active failover.\n- GraphQL or alternate transport (gRPC, WebSockets) beyond what already exists.\n- New business endpoints — Phase 3 hardens the existing surface only.\n- `/v2` and any breaking changes to the contract.\n\n## Success criteria\n\nPhase 3 is done when all of the following are true:\n\n- 100% of `/v1` routes require a valid JWT and pass through scope checks.\n- 100% of `/v1` routes are covered by the per-token rate limiter.\n- 100% of non-2xx responses conform to RFC 7807 with stable error codes.\n- Every request emits a structured log line and an OTel trace; RED metrics are visible in dashboards.\n- p95 latency and error-rate SLOs (defined in `analysis.md`) are met for two consecutive weeks in production.\n- On-call runbook is written, reviewed, and linked from the service README.\n- The first-party frontend is fully migrated to `/v1` with the legacy endpoints deprecated and traffic at zero.\n",
  "suggested_action": "run spec-pipeline"
}
